### One client API, one engine per platform

<figure class="viz"><div class="node is-key" style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong><code>commonMain</code>: ktor-client-core</strong><small><code>HttpClient</code> and the request/response pipeline; plugins for serialization, logging, auth and defaults; does not open a TCP socket</small></div><div class="node" style="max-width:20rem;margin:0 auto .6rem"><strong><code>HttpClientEngine</code> interface</strong></div><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong><code>androidMain</code>: ktor-client-okhttp</strong><small>OkHttp networking and interceptor compatibility</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong><code>iosMain</code>: ktor-client-darwin</strong><small>NSURLSession, ATS, proxy and background-download support</small></div></div><figcaption>Redrawn from the original diagram. Shared code owns request policy; each platform's engine owns the socket, TLS and OS networking rules.</figcaption></figure>
